          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My knees hurt most days and I have been told to lose weight. Is joint pain ever urgent, and what does guidance describe as core treatment?",
          "answer": "Joint pain is not usually an emergency, but some joint symptoms need prompt assessment. The risk check on your description returned a routine tier, with no emergency pattern matched. That does not rule out a serious problem. If your knee becomes hot, swollen and very painful, or you have a fever, seek urgent care. The same applies if the knee suddenly locks or gives way. (Source: Osteoarthritis, condition summary.)\n\nBased on the retrieved guidance, the core treatment for knee osteoarthritis is structured exercise. This combines muscle strengthening around the joint, range-of-movement work, and aerobic activity, usually started with supervision (Source: Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management, guideline summary). Weight management is added where relevant (Source: Osteoarthritis, condition summary). The guidance says exercise helps regardless of how worn the joint looks on imaging, and that pain during exercise within tolerable limits does not mean damage. Lower-impact options such as walking, cycling, and water-based exercise are described as useful starting points (Source: Exercising with osteoarthritis, lifestyle summary).\n\nMedicines are described as add-ons rather than the foundation of treatment. Joint replacement is considered only when pain persists despite good non-surgical care (Source: Osteoarthritis, condition summary).\n\nThe results did not cover your specific knee, your personal risk, or which treatments suit you. A clinician can assess that. It would be reasonable to ask about a supervised exercise plan and how weight management could fit in.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
